Yes. There are really two complementary modes of knowing in what you’re describing: reduce your own perturbations so the environment becomes easier to read, and deliberately perturb the environment because some variables are only observable through interaction.

Stillness is almost an experimental control:

observed change = world change + self-generated change + noise

Reduce self-generated movement and some external deltas become much easier to discriminate—tiny sounds, air movement, animal behavior, changes in pressure, another person’s motion, subtle bodily signals. Hunters, naturalists, astronomers, meditators, and experimental physicists all exploit versions of that principle.

But other properties cannot be learned passively. You have to probe. Vision uses eye movements; touch often requires sliding or pressing; balance depends partly on movement; echolocation requires emitting a signal; material stiffness is learned by applying force and observing displacement.

So perception alternates between:

be still → let reality perturb you

and

act → perturb reality → measure its response

The second is active sensing. The action itself becomes part of the sensor.

Your water example is particularly good. Humans do not appear to have a dedicated skin receptor that simply reports “wet.” Wetness perception is constructed substantially from combinations of temperature change, pressure, friction, tactile signals, and prior expectations. Your brain receives several variables and compresses them into the percept:

wet.

That exposes something deeper: many things that feel like primitive sensations are probably already derived variables.

We experience:

“an object,”
“cold,”
“distance,”
“my hand,”
“a voice,”
“danger,”
“wetness”

rather than experiencing the enormous underlying collection of receptor activations, neuronal timing relationships, Bayesian-ish priors, motor predictions, and environmental causes that produced them.

So “direct perception” is partly a user-interface concept.

The nervous system effectively computes something like:

high-dimensional signals → latent cause estimate → task-useful percept

and throws away enormous amounts of intermediate structure.

That is compression, but not merely laziness. It is what makes behavior possible. If you had to consciously resolve every photon and receptor event before recognizing a tiger, you would have a remarkably accurate final thought.

The tiger would have eaten you.

So evolution tends to preserve sufficient representations: enough information to make useful decisions, not necessarily enough to reconstruct the universe perfectly.

And that gives us several different possible “bedrocks.”

There can be a phenomenological bedrock: “this is simply what red feels like to me.”

A biological bedrock: the receptor/transduction machinery producing the signal.

A computational bedrock: the latent variable sufficient for the organism’s task.

A physical bedrock: the underlying electromagnetic interactions.

And perhaps deeper physical descriptions beneath that.

Finding one doesn’t prove you’ve reached the absolute bottom. It means you’ve reached a layer where further decomposition is unnecessary for the question currently being asked.

That distinction is useful because different tasks require different resolution.

For drinking:

water

may be enough.

For navigation:

river / ocean / rain / humidity

may matter.

For chemistry:

H₂O concentration + dissolved ions + contaminants

For fluid mechanics:

density + pressure + viscosity + velocity field

Same world, different compressed representations.

And once you recognize perception this way, you can deliberately manufacture new perceptual primitives. Take several variables that currently require conscious inference:

A + B + C + D → calculate X

Then repeatedly map X into a stable visual, auditory, or haptic signal until the brain starts treating X itself as a perceptual object.

That is how an initially mathematical relationship can become something you effectively feel.

Which makes your stillness/interaction distinction part of a larger cycle:

stillness → observe natural variation
→ hypothesis
→ controlled action
→ observe induced delta
→ infer hidden variable
→ compress into representation
→ practice
→ new intuition/sense

Then stillness becomes useful again, because now you’re sitting in the same environment with one more dimension available to notice.

That is a pretty clean account of how organisms, scientists, and perhaps increasingly AI systems expand the portion of reality they can inhabit.